45
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:26,800
The real problem with the LLM is

46
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:28,560
that it only knows what it's read on

47
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:29,160
the internet

48
00:01:29,160 --> 00:01:30,940
and it can't do anything.

49
00:01:31,020 --> 00:01:32,560
It just gives us tokens on the

50
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:35,060
output and we want to write agentic

51
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:35,680
applications

52
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:39,520
that take action and that know about

53
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:40,800
what's going on in the business.

54
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:43,420
So for our agents, we'll have some

55
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:45,040
kind of input.

56
00:01:45,060 --> 00:01:47,460
That's probably a prompt from a

57
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:48,740
human at some point.

58
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:53,660
This agent now here is going

59
00:01:53,660 --> 00:01:57,800
to make LLM calls and probably make

60
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:58,920
them iteratively.

61
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:00,460
It'll probably call an LLM more than

62
00:02:00,460 --> 00:02:01,060
once

63
00:02:01,300 --> 00:02:04,060
and it has the capability of calling

64
00:02:04,060 --> 00:02:05,620
tools as well.

65
00:02:05,620 --> 00:02:07,380
Those will let it actually take

66
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:08,840
action out in the world

67
00:02:08,840 --> 00:02:10,980
and both the LLM call and the tool

68
00:02:10,980 --> 00:02:12,720
call can be iterative.

69
00:02:13,020 --> 00:02:14,840
This agent can have a workflow that

70
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:16,580
it kind of works out over time.

71
00:02:16,580 --> 00:02:19,540
That can be running for a long time,

72
00:02:19,560 --> 00:02:21,160
potentially, depending on the agent.

73
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:23,320
And that introduces some real

74
00:02:23,320 --> 00:02:25,040
problems with context that we need

75
00:02:25,040 --> 00:02:25,640
to solve.

76
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:28,660
All right, let's look now at kind of

77
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:32,000
a schema for thinking

78
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,160
about context.

79
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:38,100
What goes in the context window?

80
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:40,680
These tokens, this big prompt,

81
00:02:40,760 --> 00:02:43,200
this string that we're submitting to

82
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:44,200
our LLM.

83
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:46,780
There are six things in here that I

84
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:47,420
want to lay out.

85
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:51,200
Let's first say, let's say here,

86
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:54,200
this is what I'm going to call user

87
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:54,800
message.

88
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:58,080
All right, now it's okay to think of

89
00:02:58,080 --> 00:02:59,140
the chatbot interface,

90
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:00,280
the typical, you know,

91
00:03:00,420 --> 00:03:02,200
Grok, Claude, ChatTPT kind of thing.

92
00:03:02,520 --> 00:03:04,560
You type a thing and it says stuff

93
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:05,160
back.

94
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:06,200
And the you type a thing,

95
00:03:06,200 --> 00:03:07,240
that stuff that you type,

96
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:08,740
that's the user message.

97
00:03:08,740 --> 00:03:11,400
And that's probably going to be the

98
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:14,060
initial input to our agentic service

99
00:03:14,060 --> 00:03:14,660
here,

100
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:16,240
some kind of user message.

101
00:03:16,820 --> 00:03:18,280
That is not all.

102
00:03:18,640 --> 00:03:22,820
There is always a system prompt.

103
00:03:23,800 --> 00:03:25,560
In the chatbot applications,

104
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:27,760
you have some capability of

105
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:28,920
customizing that system prompt.

106
00:03:29,180 --> 00:03:30,360
There's always system prompt, you're

107
00:03:30,360 --> 00:03:31,080
not going to see.

108
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:32,740
But you can always tune that up a

109
00:03:32,740 --> 00:03:33,340
little bit.

110
00:03:33,340 --> 00:03:33,940
And that's going to tell things

111
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:34,540
like,

112
00:03:34,540 --> 00:03:36,420
what's the personality of the LLM?

113
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:37,760
Like, what do you want it to be?

114
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:38,960
Do you want it to be a harsh and

115
00:03:38,960 --> 00:03:39,720
demanding coach?

116
00:03:40,080 --> 00:03:41,260
Do you want it to be sort of a

117
00:03:41,260 --> 00:03:43,560
gentle and nurturing counselor?

118
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:45,020
You know, to kind of tell it what

119
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:45,620
you want.

120
00:03:45,620 --> 00:03:46,720
Give it some guardrails.

121
00:03:47,080 --> 00:03:48,880
Like, don't give me bomb -making

122
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:49,600
instructions.

123
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:52,800
Or be specific and detailed and cite

124
00:03:52,800 --> 00:03:54,220
your sources in your output.

125
00:03:54,520 --> 00:03:55,800
These are all sorts of things that

126
00:03:55,800 --> 00:03:57,060
might go in a system prompt.

127
00:03:57,400 --> 00:03:58,920
So it's just sort of up there.

128
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:00,660
You don't have to say all that stuff

129
00:04:00,660 --> 00:04:01,520
over and over again.

130
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:02,640
It's always present.

131
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,280
We also have tools that we might

132
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:07,320
have access to.

133
00:04:07,740 --> 00:04:09,720
The whole point here is that the

134
00:04:09,720 --> 00:04:10,960
agent has access to tools.

135
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:13,520
And in its terminal step,

136
00:04:13,520 --> 00:04:15,260
when it's done with its work,

137
00:04:15,600 --> 00:04:16,400
that's a tool call.

138
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:18,080
That's causing an effect out in the

139
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:18,680
world.

140
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:20,680
So as a part of this context,

141
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:22,160
this text we're passing into the

142
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:22,760
LLM,

143
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:24,160
we have a description of the tools

144
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:25,920
that are available to us.

145
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:29,660
We also have resources.

146
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:34,200
Now this is a complex topic all

147
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:34,960
on its own.

148
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:37,260
Because the LLM only knows what it

149
00:04:37,260 --> 00:04:38,060
reads on the internet,

150
00:04:38,440 --> 00:04:40,180
we have to tell it things that are

151
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:41,460
specific to our business.

152
00:04:41,980 --> 00:04:44,960
Like, hopefully all your Google Docs

153
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:46,300
are not shared publicly.

154
00:04:46,520 --> 00:04:49,420
All of your Wiki articles and

155
00:04:49,420 --> 00:04:51,500
internal support tickets and all

156
00:04:51,500 --> 00:04:52,140
that kind of stuff.

157
00:04:52,140 --> 00:04:53,360
You know, there really is private

158
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:54,740
data inside your enterprise

159
00:04:54,740 --> 00:04:56,640
that is never going to be a part of

160
00:04:56,640 --> 00:04:58,660
any LLM's training data set.

161
00:04:59,240 --> 00:05:00,520
That's the resources.

162
00:05:00,760 --> 00:05:03,100
So we have to somehow do a good job

163
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:04,520
of picking out which resources

164
00:05:05,580 --> 00:05:07,840
belong inside this context window

165
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:10,080
into the submission to the LLM.

166
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:11,060
Those are things that are going to

167
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:12,540
be relevant to the user message.

168
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:14,940
Enough, but not too much.

169
00:05:15,260 --> 00:05:16,680
We'll talk more about that in a bit.

170
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:20,840
You've also got the assistant

171
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:21,760
messages.

172
00:05:22,980 --> 00:05:24,280
And assistant is, remember,

173
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:25,240
think of the chat model,

174
00:05:25,380 --> 00:05:25,980
right?

175
00:05:25,980 --> 00:05:26,960
You type stuff, it says stuff back.

176
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:28,940
It is your assistant.

177
00:05:29,140 --> 00:05:31,300
So you give it a user message and

178
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:32,420
then it says something back.

179
00:05:32,420 --> 00:05:34,700
That is the assistant message.

180
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:36,860
So as we iterate through,

181
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:39,180
and this is the case when you're

182
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:40,660
using a chat bot application,

183
00:05:41,020 --> 00:05:44,300
you have actually a history of user

184
00:05:44,300 --> 00:05:44,980
messages

185
00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:48,620
and a history of assistant

186
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:51,260
messages that are accumulating

187
00:05:51,260 --> 00:05:52,860
inside the context window.

188
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:55,780
So the longer the agent iterates,

189
00:05:55,860 --> 00:05:58,180
the more space this stuff takes up.

190
00:05:58,740 --> 00:06:01,840
Finally, you've got tool calls

191
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:04,120
and responses.

192
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:06,000
Call and response tools are calls

193
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:06,620
and results.

194
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:10,340
This is, as an agent is doing its

195
00:06:10,340 --> 00:06:12,300
work, it may call tools as

196
00:06:12,300 --> 00:06:13,140
intermediate steps,

197
00:06:13,400 --> 00:06:15,280
not to terminate execution and have

198
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:15,920
its full result,

199
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:19,000
but to go do other things to help

200
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:20,120
build up context

201
00:06:20,120 --> 00:06:22,860
to get to where it can be done with

202
00:06:22,860 --> 00:06:23,460
its work.

203
00:06:23,600 --> 00:06:25,380
So you may have a history of tool

204
00:06:25,380 --> 00:06:27,980
calls and responses also as a part

205
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:28,580
of this.

206
00:06:28,580 --> 00:06:29,720
These three components,

207
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:31,020
the history of tool calls,

208
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:31,800
user messages,

209
00:06:31,800 --> 00:06:32,620
and assistant messages,

210
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:35,820
these are going to grow as execution

211
00:06:35,820 --> 00:06:36,960
unfolds.

212
00:06:37,220 --> 00:06:38,900
Resources, I'm drawing it as the

213
00:06:38,900 --> 00:06:40,400
same size box as all the rest of

214
00:06:40,400 --> 00:06:41,000
them.

215
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:42,480
It might not be the same size.

216
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:45,140
That might be a substantial number

217
00:06:45,140 --> 00:06:47,120
of tokens there that are taken up by

218
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:47,720
resources.

219
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:49,300
System prompt, that's probably

220
00:06:49,300 --> 00:06:50,040
reasonable, right?

221
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:51,260
Tools, that's probably going to be

222
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:51,860
limited.

223
00:06:51,860 --> 00:06:53,100
But these other things can be a lot

224
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:53,700
of space.

225
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:54,980
You see, when you start to look at

226
00:06:54,980 --> 00:06:56,440
what really goes into context,

227
00:06:56,440 --> 00:07:00,100
you begin to understand even the big

228
00:07:00,100 --> 00:07:01,480
models with a million tokens,

229
00:07:01,540 --> 00:07:02,920
which again is not all of them.

230
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:04,960
That doesn't feel very roomy anymore

231
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:05,720
when you're thinking about

232
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:07,380
building complex, agentic

233
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:07,980
applications.

234
00:07:08,380 --> 00:07:10,260
And it gets a little worse than

235
00:07:10,260 --> 00:07:10,860
that.

236
00:07:10,860 --> 00:07:12,620
It's not always the case that more

237
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:13,220
is better.

238
00:07:13,460 --> 00:07:14,840
If you're using a model that has,

239
00:07:15,020 --> 00:07:16,760
say, a 100,000 token context window,

240
00:07:17,100 --> 00:07:18,720
it's a pretty consistent result at

241
00:07:18,720 --> 00:07:19,740
this point that

242
00:07:19,740 --> 00:07:21,880
maxing that out doesn't give you

243
00:07:21,880 --> 00:07:22,680
your best results.

244
00:07:23,140 --> 00:07:25,660
Somewhere in the 60 to 70 % range.

245
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:27,720
Again, let the research unfold on

246
00:07:27,720 --> 00:07:29,660
this and let that number stabilize.

247
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:31,680
But kind of somewhere in the 60 to

248
00:07:31,680 --> 00:07:34,320
70 % of the capacity of the context

249
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:34,920
window

250
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:37,060
is probably where you get your best

251
00:07:37,060 --> 00:07:37,660
results.

252
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:39,680
So more isn't always better.

253
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:41,600
We actually do have to have tools

254
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:43,320
for engineering what goes in here.

255
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:45,620
And let's look at what that set of

256
00:07:45,620 --> 00:07:46,460
tools is.

257
00:07:46,540 --> 00:07:48,360
There's four things I want to cover.

258
00:07:48,560 --> 00:07:52,740
First, there is the system prompt

259
00:07:52,740 --> 00:07:53,560
itself.

260
00:07:53,560 --> 00:07:55,940
Now, writing a good prompt is

261
00:07:55,940 --> 00:07:56,540
important.

262
00:07:56,540 --> 00:07:57,520
We need to get this right.

263
00:07:57,520 --> 00:07:59,260
This is a thing that is kind of a

264
00:07:59,260 --> 00:08:00,560
one -off for our agent.

265
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:02,160
And you want to nail it.

266
00:08:02,540 --> 00:08:03,840
And there is a little bit of a

267
00:08:03,840 --> 00:08:05,020
Goldilocks thing going on here.

268
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:08,260
We could, in this ditch over here,

269
00:08:08,640 --> 00:08:11,060
be too vague.

270
00:08:11,160 --> 00:08:13,360
We could say, I don't know,

271
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:14,740
do a good job

272
00:08:15,520 --> 00:08:17,580
and go get all the stuff you need to

273
00:08:17,580 --> 00:08:18,260
give me a good answer.

274
00:08:18,540 --> 00:08:19,600
That's kind of stupid,

275
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:20,320
right?

276
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:20,920
That's not really going to work.

277
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:24,500
Over here, we could be too

278
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:25,380
prescriptive.

279
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:27,940
And for example, if you find

280
00:08:27,940 --> 00:08:29,300
yourself in your system prompt

281
00:08:30,080 --> 00:08:33,600
defining kind of if -then logic and

282
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:35,220
saying make sure you do this

283
00:08:35,220 --> 00:08:37,740
and if this happens, you want to let

284
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:38,600
the LLM figure that out.

285
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:39,780
That's what it's going to be good

286
00:08:39,780 --> 00:08:40,380
at.

287
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:40,980
So don't do that.

288
00:08:40,980 --> 00:08:41,580
Don't do that.

289
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:42,900
You want to be in the middle where

290
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:44,240
you define outcomes,

291
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:46,460
you define maybe broad approaches to

292
00:08:46,460 --> 00:08:47,060
things.

293
00:08:47,140 --> 00:08:49,140
And I realize this is itself

294
00:08:49,140 --> 00:08:50,220
somewhat vague.

295
00:08:50,220 --> 00:08:52,220
But it's a balance that you're going

296
00:08:52,220 --> 00:08:53,160
to have to learn how to strike.

297
00:08:53,420 --> 00:08:55,060
And that is sort of the art of

298
00:08:55,060 --> 00:08:55,660
prompt engineering

299
00:08:55,660 --> 00:08:56,860
is making sure that you're not too

300
00:08:56,860 --> 00:08:57,820
prescriptive, not too vague.

301
00:08:58,020 --> 00:08:59,300
So get the system prompt right.

302
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:00,160
Engineer that thing.

303
00:09:00,380 --> 00:09:01,480
That's something you actually have

304
00:09:01,480 --> 00:09:02,160
to pay attention to.

305
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:03,360
What's next?

306
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:04,000
Tools.

307
00:09:05,440 --> 00:09:06,440
Pretty simple here.

308
00:09:06,660 --> 00:09:08,220
You just want to make sure your tool

309
00:09:08,220 --> 00:09:11,280
descriptions are specific and

310
00:09:11,280 --> 00:09:11,880
detailed.

311
00:09:11,940 --> 00:09:13,660
You never want them to be too long.

312
00:09:13,980 --> 00:09:15,200
More is not always better.

313
00:09:15,400 --> 00:09:17,420
But you want them to really nail

314
00:09:17,420 --> 00:09:18,960
down what does this tool do.

315
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:20,820
You want the LLM to be able to,

316
00:09:20,820 --> 00:09:22,600
as it were, understand what that

317
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:23,220
thing is for.

318
00:09:23,620 --> 00:09:25,820
So you want to be specific.

319
00:09:26,540 --> 00:09:29,020
And you want to include a schema.

320
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:30,960
We have to know what goes into that

321
00:09:30,960 --> 00:09:32,580
tool, what comes out of that tool.

322
00:09:32,600 --> 00:09:34,800
That's important because the LLM

323
00:09:34,800 --> 00:09:37,620
will need to know that it has all

324
00:09:37,620 --> 00:09:38,360
the right inputs

325
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:40,380
to be able to conclude that it can

326
00:09:40,380 --> 00:09:41,020
call the tool.

327
00:09:41,220 --> 00:09:42,840
And it's going to need to know what

328
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:43,940
it gets out of the tool

329
00:09:43,940 --> 00:09:47,020
because that may be an input to a

330
00:09:47,020 --> 00:09:49,260
next step of, say, a resource it

331
00:09:49,260 --> 00:09:49,920
needs to go get

332
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:51,000
or something like that.

333
00:09:51,520 --> 00:09:53,560
So full schemas, specific,

334
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:55,680
precise tool descriptions.

335
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:56,840
OK, what's next?

336
00:09:56,880 --> 00:09:59,500
We've got data retrieval.

337
00:09:59,960 --> 00:10:03,680
And this really goes right here to

338
00:10:03,680 --> 00:10:04,280
resources.

339
00:10:04,360 --> 00:10:05,660
This is what we're talking about.

340
00:10:06,140 --> 00:10:08,560
Now, the initial approach when folks

341
00:10:08,560 --> 00:10:10,740
just started trying to use LLMs

342
00:10:10,740 --> 00:10:12,740
in an enterprise context was the

343
00:10:12,740 --> 00:10:14,360
Retrieval Augmented Generation

344
00:10:14,360 --> 00:10:15,240
Approach, or RAG,

345
00:10:15,660 --> 00:10:17,980
where we've got a vector database.

346
00:10:18,260 --> 00:10:20,340
We index any document we think we

347
00:10:20,340 --> 00:10:23,060
might need to have involved in a

348
00:10:23,060 --> 00:10:23,680
lookup.

349
00:10:24,520 --> 00:10:26,340
And now when I've got a user

350
00:10:26,340 --> 00:10:28,340
message, I'll take that user message

351
00:10:28,340 --> 00:10:30,620
and use it in the vector database to

352
00:10:30,620 --> 00:10:32,620
go retrieve any documents I think

353
00:10:32,620 --> 00:10:33,380
are related,

354
00:10:34,060 --> 00:10:36,000
put them into the context,

355
00:10:36,760 --> 00:10:38,160
and away I go.

356
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:40,680
And that's good for an information

357
00:10:40,680 --> 00:10:42,060
search, chatbot kind of thing.

358
00:10:42,060 --> 00:10:42,680
It worked.

359
00:10:43,100 --> 00:10:44,760
And that may well still be a part of

360
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:45,360
your stack.

361
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:46,960
And I'm not here to tell you that

362
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:47,700
you're old fashioned

363
00:10:47,700 --> 00:10:48,980
or behind the times or anything.

364
00:10:49,540 --> 00:10:52,120
But RAG by itself is not very

365
00:10:52,120 --> 00:10:54,500
precise context engineering on a go

366
00:10:54,500 --> 00:10:55,100
-forward basis.

367
00:10:55,200 --> 00:10:57,600
There's more we can do to

368
00:10:57,600 --> 00:10:59,940
economically use the context window

369
00:10:59,940 --> 00:11:00,540
that we've got.

370
00:11:00,760 --> 00:11:04,820
So now we've got MCP, the Model

371
00:11:04,820 --> 00:11:05,720
Context Protocol,

372
00:11:05,720 --> 00:11:09,340
and its disclosure of resources that

373
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:10,380
can be queried.

374
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:13,260
Those resources, like tools,

375
00:11:13,440 --> 00:11:15,440
are described with text

376
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:17,600
and maybe with parameters that can

377
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:18,700
be involved in a query

378
00:11:18,700 --> 00:11:20,560
if they're queryable resources.

379
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:24,580
And so those descriptions can go

380
00:11:24,580 --> 00:11:27,260
into the context window of one call.

381
00:11:27,880 --> 00:11:29,060
And a subsequent call,

382
00:11:29,180 --> 00:11:31,360
if you ask the LLM, can say,

383
00:11:31,440 --> 00:11:33,100
hey, you know what, I need you to go

384
00:11:33,100 --> 00:11:34,280
query this resource.

385
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:36,440
That can even be, there's this

386
00:11:36,440 --> 00:11:37,160
little arrow here

387
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:39,140
where this agent is making multiple

388
00:11:39,140 --> 00:11:39,840
iterative calls.

389
00:11:40,300 --> 00:11:43,500
One of those calls could be simply

390
00:11:43,500 --> 00:11:44,280
to ask,

391
00:11:44,380 --> 00:11:46,140
based on this user message,

392
00:11:46,620 --> 00:11:48,740
which resources should I go

393
00:11:48,740 --> 00:11:49,340
retrieve?

394
00:11:50,180 --> 00:11:52,060
And the LLM will tell you,

395
00:11:52,060 --> 00:11:53,900
based on its conclusion,

396
00:11:54,340 --> 00:11:56,020
you go get those resources.

397
00:11:56,320 --> 00:11:57,600
Now you've got a big string.

398
00:11:57,820 --> 00:11:59,760
You can dump that into the resources

399
00:11:59,760 --> 00:12:00,360
section

400
00:12:00,360 --> 00:12:02,760
of your next context window for your

401
00:12:02,760 --> 00:12:03,360
next call

402
00:12:03,360 --> 00:12:05,040
as the agent moves along.

403
00:12:05,440 --> 00:12:06,980
There are other things we can do to

404
00:12:06,980 --> 00:12:08,520
make economical use of this,

405
00:12:08,740 --> 00:12:11,900
like say, if you've got a user

406
00:12:11,900 --> 00:12:13,740
record that could be kind of long,

407
00:12:14,100 --> 00:12:15,720
maybe just give it a user ID.

408
00:12:16,220 --> 00:12:18,200
And if there's a resource or a tool

409
00:12:18,200 --> 00:12:18,800
or something

410
00:12:18,800 --> 00:12:20,940
that clearly can turn that user ID

411
00:12:20,940 --> 00:12:22,180
into a full record

412
00:12:22,180 --> 00:12:24,340
on a subsequent call, you can get

413
00:12:24,340 --> 00:12:25,740
the model to ask you.

414
00:12:26,040 --> 00:12:28,260
You can prompt it to ask you if it

415
00:12:28,260 --> 00:12:30,340
needs more detail on that user,

416
00:12:30,460 --> 00:12:31,960
call this tool or ask for this

417
00:12:31,960 --> 00:12:32,560
resource.

418
00:12:32,560 --> 00:12:34,540
So there are all kinds of things we

419
00:12:34,540 --> 00:12:35,220
can do here

420
00:12:35,220 --> 00:12:39,180
to just be economical with our

421
00:12:39,180 --> 00:12:40,300
data that we have in here.

422
00:12:40,300 --> 00:12:41,660
Rather than anything that might

423
00:12:41,660 --> 00:12:42,740
sound like it's related,

424
00:12:43,040 --> 00:12:44,500
we can just sort of describe what's

425
00:12:44,500 --> 00:12:45,100
available

426
00:12:45,100 --> 00:12:48,180
and use prompting to have the model

427
00:12:48,180 --> 00:12:49,660
tell us

428
00:12:49,660 --> 00:12:51,540
what we need to go get and include.

429
00:12:52,120 --> 00:12:55,740
Finally, we've got the long horizon

430
00:12:55,740 --> 00:12:56,400
thinking.

431
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:58,520
And this is for an agent that we

432
00:12:58,520 --> 00:12:59,120
know

433
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:02,300
is going to be really iterating for

434
00:13:02,300 --> 00:13:02,960
a long time.

435
00:13:03,020 --> 00:13:04,580
We suspect that it might need to

436
00:13:04,580 --> 00:13:05,540
make a bunch of calls.

437
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:09,740
And these things that grow as the

438
00:13:09,740 --> 00:13:10,940
agent's runtime increases,

439
00:13:11,120 --> 00:13:12,260
those are going to be our enemy.

440
00:13:12,620 --> 00:13:13,900
And so there are some things that we

441
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:14,500
can do

442
00:13:14,500 --> 00:13:16,560
to help keep those small,

443
00:13:16,640 --> 00:13:17,780
or at least within the context

444
00:13:17,780 --> 00:13:19,240
window of any given call.

445
00:13:19,740 --> 00:13:21,140
First is compaction.

446
00:13:21,840 --> 00:13:23,700
One thing LLMs are incredible at is

447
00:13:23,700 --> 00:13:24,620
summarizing text.

448
00:13:24,880 --> 00:13:26,520
If I've got some resource I've

449
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:28,060
retrieved on a previous call

450
00:13:28,060 --> 00:13:31,260
and it's, you know, 50,000 tokens or

451
00:13:31,260 --> 00:13:31,860
something,

452
00:13:31,860 --> 00:13:32,840
a really big thing, you know,

453
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,300
I don't want to have all of that in

454
00:13:35,300 --> 00:13:36,060
my next call.

455
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:39,060
So I could use a single LLM call

456
00:13:39,060 --> 00:13:40,480
with my own prompt

457
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:43,960
to say, please summarize this in 500

458
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:44,560
words.

459
00:13:44,660 --> 00:13:46,200
And now I've got that compacted

460
00:13:46,200 --> 00:13:46,800
summary

461
00:13:46,800 --> 00:13:47,720
that goes in as a resource.

462
00:13:47,960 --> 00:13:49,060
That can often give you pretty good

463
00:13:49,060 --> 00:13:49,660
results.

464
00:13:51,180 --> 00:13:52,880
Next is memory.

465
00:13:53,900 --> 00:13:55,600
Memory is literally just,

466
00:13:55,600 --> 00:13:56,800
it's going to be a little key value

467
00:13:56,800 --> 00:13:57,400
store

468
00:13:57,400 --> 00:13:58,840
hanging off to the side here.

469
00:14:00,720 --> 00:14:03,640
And that will let me at one point in

470
00:14:03,640 --> 00:14:04,840
my agent's execution

471
00:14:04,840 --> 00:14:07,460
take some resource, some

472
00:14:07,460 --> 00:14:08,460
intermediate result,

473
00:14:08,580 --> 00:14:09,740
maybe an assistant message

474
00:14:09,740 --> 00:14:11,380
that itself could be somewhat

475
00:14:11,380 --> 00:14:11,980
lengthy.

476
00:14:11,980 --> 00:14:13,240
Maybe it's some kind of structured

477
00:14:13,240 --> 00:14:13,840
data.

478
00:14:13,840 --> 00:14:15,600
Maybe it's like a bunch of JSON or

479
00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:16,200
CSV

480
00:14:16,200 --> 00:14:17,780
or who knows what's going on

481
00:14:17,780 --> 00:14:19,540
with this crazy agent you're

482
00:14:19,540 --> 00:14:20,140
writing.

483
00:14:20,300 --> 00:14:23,900
And we can put that in here under a

484
00:14:23,900 --> 00:14:24,520
certain key

485
00:14:24,520 --> 00:14:26,460
and on a subsequent step when we

486
00:14:26,460 --> 00:14:27,060
need it,

487
00:14:27,060 --> 00:14:28,480
retrieve it out of that memory.

488
00:14:29,160 --> 00:14:31,280
So it's conceptually like what

489
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:31,880
happens

490
00:14:31,880 --> 00:14:33,680
with retrieving resources from an

491
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:34,380
MCP server,

492
00:14:34,700 --> 00:14:36,980
but it really is memory that's local

493
00:14:36,980 --> 00:14:37,920
to our agent

494
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:40,460
and that we get to use to store that

495
00:14:40,460 --> 00:14:41,060
stuff.

496
00:14:41,060 --> 00:14:42,460
You don't have much space here.

497
00:14:42,680 --> 00:14:44,300
You might need to put certain things

498
00:14:44,300 --> 00:14:45,100
over here

499
00:14:45,100 --> 00:14:46,940
where this space is relatively

500
00:14:46,940 --> 00:14:47,540
cheap.

501
00:14:48,380 --> 00:14:51,000
Finally, we have, I'm just going to

502
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:51,600
say,

503
00:14:52,280 --> 00:14:53,640
additional agents.

504
00:14:55,100 --> 00:14:55,700
Composition.

505
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:57,880
I can take my one big agent,

506
00:14:57,980 --> 00:14:59,680
and I think this is a thing that is

507
00:14:59,680 --> 00:15:00,520
just likely to happen

508
00:15:00,520 --> 00:15:01,600
as you're building an agentic

509
00:15:01,600 --> 00:15:02,200
application.

510
00:15:02,680 --> 00:15:04,000
Tell me if this has ever happened

511
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:05,320
when you're building a microservice

512
00:15:05,320 --> 00:15:06,340
and it starts to get a little

513
00:15:06,340 --> 00:15:06,940
complex

514
00:15:06,940 --> 00:15:08,700
and you realize it's a little bit of

515
00:15:08,700 --> 00:15:09,300
its own

516
00:15:09,300 --> 00:15:09,900
little mini monolith

517
00:15:09,900 --> 00:15:10,820
and you have to split it into

518
00:15:10,820 --> 00:15:11,420
pieces.

519
00:15:11,540 --> 00:15:12,840
This is what it's like to build

520
00:15:12,840 --> 00:15:13,440
software.

521
00:15:13,440 --> 00:15:14,340
When you're writing an agent,

522
00:15:14,420 --> 00:15:15,840
it's probably going to end up

523
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:16,580
decomposing

524
00:15:16,580 --> 00:15:17,720
into multiple agents.

525
00:15:18,140 --> 00:15:20,360
Let's say one of the resources

526
00:15:20,360 --> 00:15:21,060
you've got

527
00:15:21,060 --> 00:15:23,440
is your documentation site

528
00:15:23,440 --> 00:15:25,420
or your wiki or something like that,

529
00:15:25,420 --> 00:15:26,960
and you've got some kind of complex

530
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:27,660
search logic

531
00:15:27,660 --> 00:15:28,840
that's doing a lot of iteration.

532
00:15:29,020 --> 00:15:30,620
Man, that's just its own agent.

533
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:31,780
So split that off,

534
00:15:31,980 --> 00:15:33,560
and now this agent can ask that

535
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:34,160
agent,

536
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:36,080
I've got this text.

537
00:15:36,160 --> 00:15:38,020
I'd like to know what documents

538
00:15:38,020 --> 00:15:40,000
from your system look relevant.

539
00:15:40,420 --> 00:15:42,100
And now rather than just a text

540
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:42,700
search

541
00:15:42,700 --> 00:15:43,760
on that document store,

542
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:44,860
you can have an agent

543
00:15:44,860 --> 00:15:46,260
that does some kind of thoughtful

544
00:15:46,260 --> 00:15:47,680
sorting through things and

545
00:15:47,680 --> 00:15:48,360
prioritizing

546
00:15:48,360 --> 00:15:50,340
and back and forth with an LLM

547
00:15:50,340 --> 00:15:52,160
to do a good job of giving you

548
00:15:52,160 --> 00:15:53,960
some concise summary of what comes

549
00:15:53,960 --> 00:15:54,640
from that store.

550
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,260
So be ready to take little chunks

551
00:15:57,260 --> 00:15:59,320
of agentic functionality in your

552
00:15:59,320 --> 00:15:59,920
agent

553
00:15:59,920 --> 00:16:00,720
that really could live on their own

554
00:16:00,720 --> 00:16:02,380
and make them their own thing.

555
00:16:03,060 --> 00:16:04,000
So there you go.

556
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:05,360
We've got a schema for context,

557
00:16:05,580 --> 00:16:07,500
the user message, system prompt,

558
00:16:07,940 --> 00:16:09,360
whatever resources we can cobble

559
00:16:09,360 --> 00:16:09,960
together,

560
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:11,320
listing of the tools,

561
00:16:11,580 --> 00:16:13,420
the assistant messages we get

562
00:16:13,420 --> 00:16:14,780
from our back and forth with the

563
00:16:14,780 --> 00:16:15,380
agent,

564
00:16:15,380 --> 00:16:17,160
and of course a record of the tools

565
00:16:17,160 --> 00:16:17,920
that we've called.

566
00:16:18,480 --> 00:16:19,180
That's a lot.

567
00:16:19,240 --> 00:16:20,160
That's all got to fit

568
00:16:20,160 --> 00:16:21,420
into the LLM's context window,

569
00:16:21,420 --> 00:16:23,080
and those aren't getting a lot

570
00:16:23,080 --> 00:16:23,680
bigger.

571
00:16:24,320 --> 00:16:25,780
So we have these tools,

572
00:16:26,120 --> 00:16:27,380
engineer your system prompt,

573
00:16:27,700 --> 00:16:28,860
describe your tools well,

574
00:16:29,400 --> 00:16:31,180
retrieve data intelligently,

575
00:16:31,380 --> 00:16:33,800
use this resources section wisely,

576
00:16:34,340 --> 00:16:36,640
and for long horizon iterative

577
00:16:36,640 --> 00:16:37,240
execution,

578
00:16:37,380 --> 00:16:39,120
you've got these tools available to

579
00:16:39,120 --> 00:16:39,720
you.

580
00:16:39,860 --> 00:16:42,140
Context engineering is the present

581
00:16:42,140 --> 00:16:44,180
and future of agentic AI

582
00:16:44,180 --> 00:16:44,780
development.

583
00:16:44,940 --> 00:16:46,600
I encourage you to put these tools

584
00:16:46,600 --> 00:16:47,300
